{
  "id": "longest-increasing-subsequence",
  "title": "Longest Increasing Subsequence",
  "difficulty": "medium",
  "categories": ["dynamic-programming", "binary-search"],
  "descriptionMd": "Given an integer array `nums`, find the length of its **longest strictly increasing subsequence**.\n\nA subsequence keeps the original order of the elements but may skip any of them. *Strictly* increasing means every element is greater than the one before it, so equal values cannot both be used.\n\n**Example 1**\n\n```\nnums = [10, 9, 2, 5, 3, 7, 101, 18]\n→ 4\n```\nOne longest choice is `2, 3, 7, 101`.\n\n**Example 2**\n\n```\nnums = [0, 1, 0, 3, 2, 3]\n→ 4\n```\n`0, 1, 2, 3`.\n\n**Example 3**\n\n```\nnums = [7, 7, 7, 7, 7, 7, 7]\n→ 1\n```\n\n**Follow-up:** an O(n²) solution is straightforward — can you reach O(n log n)?",
  "constraints": ["1 ≤ nums.length ≤ 2500", "-10⁴ ≤ nums[i] ≤ 10⁴"],
  "hints": ["Let `best[i]` be the length of the longest increasing subsequence that ends exactly at index `i`. How does it relate to earlier entries?", "For the faster approach, keep, for every possible length, the smallest value that an increasing subsequence of that length can end with. That list stays sorted."],
  "signature": {
    "functionName": "lengthOfLIS",
    "params": [{ "name": "nums", "type": "int[]" }],
    "returns": "int"
  },
  "comparator": "exact",
